<Base
  title="About — Marissa Bellas"
  description="Marissa Bellas has worked in delivery, sales, and product on security and AI. This is the field guide that came out of it."
>
  <h1 class="thesis thesis--page">
    <span>About</span>
  </h1>

  <div class="prose hero-fade">
    <p>
      I'm Marissa Bellas. I've worked on security and AI from three seats, and this site exists
      because the view from each one contradicts the others in ways that turned out to matter.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Delivery first.</strong> I was on the deployment side, which is where you learn that
      the gap between a control on a slide and a control an operator will actually run is where
      most security programs quietly fail.
      {/* SLOT: one concrete sentence — the kind of environments, the scale, the years.
          Something only you can say. No employer name needed. */}
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Then sales.</strong> Sales teaches you a thing delivery cannot: the difference
      between what a customer asks for, what they will approve budget for, and what they will
      still be running in eighteen months. Those are three different products.
      {/* SLOT: one line on what you sold and to whom, in categories not brand names. */}
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Now customer experience.</strong> Product-development adjacent — close enough to the
      people building it to be heard, far enough downstream to see what customers actually run
      into once the deal is signed and the pilot is over. It's a useful vantage and an
      uncomfortable one. You find out how much of what you carried as a seller was real, how much
      was a symptom of something else, and how much of what gets built answers a question nobody
      in the field was asking.
      {/* SLOT: what you work on now, at the category level — the kinds of
          customers, the kind of product surface. */}
    </p>

    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>
      A field guide rather than a blog. The difference is structure: a feed buries everything
      older than a month, and a guide is meant to be returned to. It's organized by what you came
      for — a situation, a practice, a failure, or something arriving next — not by when I
      happened to write it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="on-customer-stories">On customer stories</h2>
    <p>
      Everything drawn from real work is composited. Details from separate engagements get
      recombined, numbers are rounded or synthesized, and identifying specifics are removed or
      changed. Where a story needs a concrete setting, I use a fictional one and say so —
      <a href="/writing/state-of-zava/">the State of Zava</a> is the recurring example.
    </p>
    <p>
      This isn't hedging. Vague anonymization — "a large customer in a regulated industry" — is
      worse writing and worse reading. A named composite lets the specifics stay sharp while the
      client stays unidentifiable. Anything with the composite marker at the top follows that
      convention.
    </p>

    <h2>Disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      I work in customer experience in this industry, and I write about the space my employer
      sells into. Everything here is my own view, written on my own time, and does not represent
      my employer. I don't write about my own products, I don't publish anything I learned under
      NDA or in a customer escalation, and where a piece touches a category my employer competes
      in, I say so in the piece.
    </p>

